ow free river, low risk.\n- Bet (raise ≥100): try to extract value or bluff.\n- All-in (-2): aggressive, ~19,500 chips.\n\nBut your hand is weak-medium: bottom pair, decent kicker. But many hands beat you:\n- Any 8, 6, 10 (top/middle pair)\n- Any set\n- Two pair\n- Straight (if 7-9 or 9-J later, but not yet)\n\nHowever, **opponent checked behind twice** — very passive. Likely doesn’t have strong made hand. Could have:\n- Air (missed draws)\n- Underpair (e.g., 4-4, 3-3)\n- Weak top pair (e.g., A-8, K-8?) but less likely\n\nGiven the passivity, **checking back is safe** and lets you see river without committing more chips.\n\nAlternatively, **small bet (e.g., 100)** could work as a probe — if opponent folds worse hands (like J-high, Q-high), you win the pot immediately.\n\nBut risk: if opponent has better hand (e.g., 8-x), they may call or raise, putting you in tough spot.\n\nGiven stack size (~19,500), betting big isn’t necessary. Min-raise 100 could be good value bet or bluff.\n\nBut: you have **some showdown value** — your hand may win at showdown unimproved.\n\nAlso, **you\'re in the lead overall**, so avoiding unnecessary risk is wise.\n\nBest approach: **Check (0)** to control pot size and evaluate on river.\n\nWhy not bet?\n- Small chance of folding better hands.\n- Risk getting raised by better hands.\n- Little fold equity given opponent’s calling range.\n\nChecking keeps pot manageable.\n\nReason:  \nOpponent has shown consistent passivity (checked flop and turn). Your hand has marginal strength but some showdown value. Betting risks building the pot with a vulnerable hand. Checking preserves your position and allows better decisions on the river. Given you are ahead in the match, minimizing variance is prudent.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
